[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Technical Tutorial: Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) and Diffusion Models in Computer Vision"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generative modeling in computer vision has evolved rapidly over the past decade. Two dominant paradigms have emerged: Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) and Diffusion Models. While GANs rely on a competitive game-theoretic approach between a generator and a discriminator, diffusion models utilize iterative noise removal, mimicking thermodynamic processes to generate high-fidelity images."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Architecture of GANs"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"A standard GAN consists of two neural networks competing against each other:"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Generator (G): Maps random noise vector z from a prior distribution p_z(z) to the data space G(z; theta_g)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Discriminator (D): Outputs a scalar probability D(x; theta_d) representing whether x came from the training data or the generator."},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The minimax optimization objective is defined mathematically as:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"min_G max_D V(D, G) = E_x~p_data(x)[log D(x)] + E_z~p_z(z)[log(1 - D(G(z)))]"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Architecture of Diffusion Models"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Diffusion models define a forward Markov chain that gradually adds Gaussian noise to an image over T steps, and a learned reverse process that removes the noise."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Forward Process (Diffusion): q(x_t | x_{t-1}) = N(x_t; sqrt(1 - beta_t) * x_{t-1}, beta_t * I)"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reverse Process (Denoising): p_theta(x_{t-1} | x_t) = N(x_{t-1}; mu_theta(x_t, t), Sigma_theta(x_t, t))"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation Example: Simple PyTorch Discriminator"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is a basic implementation of a convolutional discriminator network used in DCGAN architectures."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"import torch
import torch.nn as nn

class Discriminator(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, channels=3, features=64):
        super(Discriminator, self).__init__()
        self.net = nn.Sequential(
            nn.Conv2d(channels, features, kernel_size=4, stride=2, padding=1),
            nn.LeakyReLU(0.2, inplace=True),
            nn.Conv2d(features, features * 2, kernel_size=4, stride=2, padding=1),
            nn.BatchNorm2d(features * 2),
            nn.LeakyReLU(0.2, inplace=True),
            nn.Conv2d(features * 2, 1, kernel_size=4, stride=2, padding=0),
            nn.Sigmoid()
        );

    def forward(self, x):
        return self.net(x).view(-1, 1).squeeze(1)"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparative Analysis"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Training Stability: GANs are notoriously unstable and prone to mode collapse. Diffusion models are trained using stable regression objectives (predicting added noise)."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sampling Speed: GANs synthesize images in a single forward pass, making them extremely fast. Diffusion models require iterative sampling (often 50 to 1000 steps), resulting in slower inference times."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sample Quality and Diversity: Modern diffusion models generally outperform GANs in terms of image diversity and Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) benchmarks."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"While GANs pioneered real-time high-resolution image generation, diffusion models currently dominate state-of-the-art computer vision tasks due to their robust training dynamics and superior sample diversity. Understanding both paradigms is crucial for modern computer vision engineers."}]
